<DocsLayout
  title="Azure security collector"
  description="What om scan azure collects from virtual machines, storage accounts, and subscription role assignments, and how public IPs become REACHABLE edges."
>
  <h1>Azure security collector</h1>
  <p>
    <code>om scan azure</code> requires <code>AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID</code>. Authentication is
    <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code>: Azure CLI (<code>az login</code>), environment variables
    (<code>AZURE_TENANT_ID</code>, <code>AZURE_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>),
    or a managed identity. The scan covers one subscription. Network security groups, Key Vault,
    SQL, and resource-group role assignments are not inventoried.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`export AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
az login
./om scan azure
./om rules run`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>AZURE_LOCATION</code> (default <code>eastus</code>) is the location segment in every
    node id, and the fallback region when a resource has none. A VM’s own location is stored on
    the node’s <code>region</code> field, but it does not change the id. Two scans with different
    <code>AZURE_LOCATION</code> values write different ids for the same VM.
  </p>

  <h2>Resources</h2>
  <div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>API</th>
          <th>Graph</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Resource groups, then virtual machines in each group</td>
          <td><code>Workload</code>. Properties: <code>resource_id</code> (ARM id) and <code>public_ip</code> (the address string, or empty).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>NIC and public IP address reads for each VM</td>
          <td>Used only to fill <code>public_ip</code>. A non-empty address adds <code>REACHABLE</code> from <code>internet:global</code>. NSGs are not read, so a public IP is treated as internet-facing even when a security rule would block it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Storage accounts and blob containers</td>
          <td><code>Datastore</code> with <code>resource_id</code> and <code>public_access</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Role assignments on the subscription</td>
          <td><code>Identity</code> named by principal id, with <code>role_definition_id</code> and <code>admin_access</code>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Node ids are <code>{'azure:{subscription}:{AZURE_LOCATION}:{kind}:{name}'}</code>.
    <code>kind</code> is <code>workload</code>, <code>datastore</code>, or <code>identity</code>.
    The identity resource is the principal id, so two assignments for the same principal collapse
    to one node. The last assignment the scan sees wins, including its <code>admin_access</code>
    flag. A principal that is both Reader and Owner can be stored as non-admin if the Reader
    assignment is processed later.
  </p>

  <h2>When storage is public</h2>
  <p>
    <code>public_access</code> is true only when both of these hold:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>allowBlobPublicAccess</code> is unset or true. An explicit false stops the check. The account is not public.</li>
    <li>At least one blob container has public access <code>blob</code> or <code>container</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    An account that allows public blobs but has no container set to blob or container access is
    stored with <code>public_access: false</code>. A missing resource group on the storage account
    id fails the scan.
  </p>

  <h2>Admin identities</h2>
  <p>
    <code>admin_access</code> is true when the role definition id contains <code>owner</code>,
    <code>contributor</code>, <code>admin</code>, or <code>useraccessadministrator</code>, compared
    case-insensitively. Built-in Owner, Contributor, and User Access Administrator match because
    those words appear in the definition id. A custom role whose id does not contain those
    substrings does not, even if the role can do the same thing.
  </p>

  <h2>Heuristic edges</h2>
  <p>After the lists return, the collector adds <code>CAN_ACCESS</code> edges:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>From every identity with <code>admin_access: true</code> to every datastore with <code>public_access: true</code>.</li>
    <li>From every workload that already has a <code>REACHABLE</code> edge to those same public datastores.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These edges are not an evaluation of RBAC on the storage account. A Contributor on the
    subscription is linked to every public storage account in that subscription, and an
    internet-facing VM is linked to those accounts even when the VM’s identity cannot read them.
    Blast radius and <code>cspm-admin-datastore-access</code> repeat that link. Private storage
    accounts get no <code>CAN_ACCESS</code> edge from this collector.
  </p>

  <h2>Read access</h2>
  <p>
    The scan lists resource groups, virtual machines, storage accounts, blob containers, and
    subscription role assignments, and it reads network interfaces and public IP addresses. A
    subscription Reader covers the ARM calls. Listing containers also needs
    <code>Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/blobServices/containers/read</code>. The collector
    does not call mutating APIs.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
